1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,600
In 1973, at UC Berkeley, a program called SPICE
learned to simulate circuits.

2
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,800
Punch cards in, line printer out.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:14,200
Fifty years later, a lot of students still learn electronics
without a lab, without an oscilloscope,

4
00:00:14,200 --> 00:00:19,800
without a simulator licence.
So we summoned both ghosts back.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,600
This is Circuit Séance. A vision model reads
a hand-drawn circuit and writes a SPICE netlist.

6
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:32,400
Then ngspice, the open-source heir of Berkeley SPICE
compiled to WebAssembly,

7
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:37,800
simulates it right here in the browser.
No server, no install.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,600
The waveform lands on a green-phosphor oscilloscope,
and its resident spirit explains it,

9
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:51,800
using numbers measured from the simulation:
tau is one millisecond, exactly R times C.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:58,600
Change a part and press Control-Enter.
The spirit re-reads the new curve,

11
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:09,600
and the homework check tells you if your hand calculation
is within five percent.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,800
Haunted Classics brings back circuits from old pages.
The 555 timer from 1972,

13
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:22,400
its capacitor sawing between one third
and two thirds of the supply.

14
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:28,400
And Bill Hewlett's 1939 Wien-bridge oscillator,
the circuit behind HP's first product,

15
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:33,800
growing a sine wave out of a ten-millivolt whisper.

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,800
Everything works in Chinese too, and on a phone,

17
00:01:39,800 --> 00:01:45,800
because the simulator runs on your own device.

18
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:51,600
Bring your own key, Qwen-VL by default,
to read your own drawings and ask a live spirit.

19
00:01:51,600 --> 00:01:55,600
The key stays in your browser.

20
00:01:55,600 --> 00:01:59,800
The demo needs no key at all,
and makes zero network requests.

21
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,400
We built it spec-first with Kiro. Kiro wrote the steering,
a requirements spec in EARS notation,

22
00:02:06,400 --> 00:02:13,800
a design and a linked task list,
then implemented it in headless task waves.

23
00:02:13,800 --> 00:02:21,600
Agent hooks fired on every save:
lint, netlist tests,

24
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:31,800
and a spirit-tone hook that reviews the LLM code
for leaked keys.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:38,400
When the free credits ran out, we finished the scope,
the spirit and the UI by hand,

26
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:43,800
and logged every hand-written file. Next, Kiro builds
a SPICE MCP server so it can simulate circuits itself.

27
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:51,800
Circuit Séance.
Berkeley SPICE, resurrected in your browser.
